  <!-- ====== CHIP COMPARISON ====== -->
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="mono-eyebrow mb-2">// who verifies what</p>
    <h2 class="glitch mb-4 text-xl font-semibold text-brand-text" data-text="Common chips compared">Common chips compared</h2>

    <div class="mb-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[1100px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-brand-border text-left whitespace-nowrap text-brand-text">
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Chip</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Used in</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">CPU</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Boot ROM verifies</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Root key in</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">TrustZone</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">TF-A</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Public break</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="align-top text-brand-body">
          {chips.map((c, i) => (
            <tr class={i < chips.length - 1 ? "border-b border-brand-border/60" : ""}>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">
                <span class="font-medium text-brand-text">{c.chip}</span>
                <span class="mt-1 block text-xs text-brand-muted">
                  {c.src.map(([label, url], j) => (
                    <>{j > 0 && ", "}<a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>{label}</a></>
                  ))}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">
                {c.used.map(([text, url], j) => (
                  <>{j > 0 && ", "}<a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>{text}</a></>
                ))}
              </td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">{c.cpu}</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">{c.rom}</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">{c.key}</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">{c.tz}</td>
              <td class="py-2 pr-4">{c.tfa}</td>
              <td class="py-2">
                {c.brk.length === 0 ? "none" : c.brk.map(([text, url], j) => (
                  <>{j > 0 && "; "}<a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>{text}</a></>
                ))}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-brand-muted">
      Entries link to their sources; technical columns are sourced under each chip name. &ldquo;Used in&rdquo; lists
      examples; &ldquo;none&rdquo; means none known or not applicable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== BIOS AND UEFI ====== -->
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="mono-eyebrow mb-2">// BL33 --&gt; firmware you know</p>
    <h2 class="glitch mb-4 text-xl font-semibold text-brand-text" data-text="BIOS and UEFI">BIOS and UEFI</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 text-brand-body">
      TF-A's chain ends at BL33, which is often a UEFI firmware: the same interface a PC uses to boot. A PC has no TF-A.
      Intel Boot Guard or AMD's Platform Secure Boot plays the boot ROM's role, and the UEFI firmware itself does BL2's
      job. Before UEFI, PCs booted with a BIOS, which verified nothing.
    </p>

    <figure class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-white p-4 sm:p-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 430" class="soc-svg mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-[980px] min-w-[860px]" role="img" aria-labelledby="pc-title pc-desc">
        <title id="pc-title">The same five boot roles on an Arm SoC with TF-A, a PC with UEFI, and a PC with a legacy BIOS</title>
        <desc id="pc-desc">Three rows of five boxes. Columns: root of trust, early init and DRAM, drivers and boot menu, OS loader, kernel. Arm with TF-A: boot ROM or BL1, BL2, BL33 running UEFI or U-Boot, an OS loader, the kernel. PC with UEFI: Intel Boot Guard or AMD Platform Secure Boot, SEC and PEI, DXE and BDS, shim and GRUB or the Windows Boot Manager, the kernel. In both rows each stage verifies the next. PC with BIOS: the reset vector, POST, loading the MBR from sector 0, the MBR and volume boot record, the kernel, with nothing verified.</desc>
        <defs>
          <marker id="abig3" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M1.5 1L8.5 5L1.5 9" fill="none" stroke="context-stroke" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        {laneCols.map((c, i) => (
          <text class="divider-label" x={laneX[i] + 75} y="50" text-anchor="middle">{c}</text>
        ))}

        {lanes.map((lane) => (
          <g>
            <text class="th" x="16" y={lane.y + 32} dominant-baseline="central" fill="#1e293b">{lane.name}</text>
            <text class="ts" x="16" y={lane.y + 50} dominant-baseline="central" fill="#64748b">{lane.sub}</text>
            {lane.boxes.map(([cls, title, l1, l2], i) => (
              <g class={cls}>
                <rect x={laneX[i]} y={lane.y} width="150" height="80" rx="10" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray={cls === "c-gray" ? "5 3" : undefined}/>
                <text class="th" x={laneX[i] + 75} y={lane.y + 24} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">{title}</text>
                <text class="ts" x={laneX[i] + 75} y={lane.y + 46} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">{l1}</text>
                <text class="ts" x={laneX[i] + 75} y={lane.y + 62} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">{l2}</text>
              </g>
            ))}
            {lane.verified.map((v, i) => (
              <line x1={laneX[i] + 151} y1={lane.y + 40} x2={laneX[i + 1] - 2} y2={lane.y + 40}
                stroke={v ? "#a32d2d" : "#94a3b8"} stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray={v ? undefined : "4 3"} marker-end="url(#abig3)"/>
            ))}
          </g>
        ))}

        <line x1="130" y1="406" x2="164" y2="406" stroke="#a32d2d" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#abig3)"/>
        <text class="ts" x="174" y="406" dominant-baseline="central" fill="#475569" style="font-size:11px">checks the next stage's signature first (when enabled)</text>
        <line x1="560" y1="406" x2="594" y2="406" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#abig3)"/>
        <text class="ts" x="604" y="406" dominant-baseline="central" fill="#475569" style="font-size:11px">loads it without any check</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-brand-muted">
        Sources:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/software-security-guidance/resources/key-usage-in-integrated-firmware-images.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Intel Boot Guard key usage</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/products/processors/technologies/amd-pro-technologies-security-white-paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>AMD Platform Secure Boot</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://www.tianocore.org/tianocore-wiki.github.io/development/design-architecture/pi_boot_flow.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>UEFI PI boot flow</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://arm-software.github.io/ebbr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Arm EBBR</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://wiki.osdev.org/MBR_(x86)" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>MBR (x86)</a>.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-semibold text-brand-text">The x86 root of trust</h3>
    <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-brand-body">
      Before any PC firmware runs, something the OEM cannot change checks it. Intel spreads the check over signed
      manifests; AMD gives it to a separate security processor. Both are the same idea as BL1 checking BL2 against a key
      hash in OTP.
    </p>

    <figure class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-white p-4 sm:p-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 620" class="soc-svg mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-[980px] min-w-[860px]" role="img" aria-labelledby="rot-title rot-desc">
        <title id="rot-title">Hardware roots of trust on Intel, AMD and Arm</title>
        <desc id="rot-desc">Three chains, each stage checking the next before it runs. Intel Boot Guard: the CPU microcode checks the Intel-signed Startup ACM; the ACM checks the OEM Key Manifest against an OEM key hash in chipset fuses, then the Boot Policy Manifest, then the Initial Boot Block against the hash in that manifest; the IBB checks the rest of the firmware. AMD Platform Secure Boot: the AMD Secure Processor boot ROM checks the ASP boot loader, which initialises silicon and DRAM and checks the OEM BIOS against an OEM key bound by fuses. Arm with TF-A: the boot ROM or BL1 checks BL2 against the ROTPK hash in OTP, and BL2 checks the BL3x images.</desc>
        <defs>
          <marker id="abig4" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M1.5 1L8.5 5L1.5 9" fill="none" stroke="context-stroke" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        {rotRows.map((row) => (
          <g>
            <text class="th" x="16" y={row.y + 26} dominant-baseline="central" fill="#1e293b">{row.name}</text>
            <text class="ts" x="16" y={row.y + 44} dominant-baseline="central" fill="#64748b" style="font-size:11px">{row.sub}</text>
            {row.boxes.map(([cls, title, l1, l2], i) => (
              <g class={cls}>
                <rect x={rotX(i)} y={row.y} width="120" height="70" rx="10" stroke-width="1.2"/>
                <text class="th" x={rotX(i) + 60} y={row.y + 20} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="font-size:13px">{title}</text>
                <text class="ts" x={rotX(i) + 60} y={row.y + 40} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="font-size:10.5px">{l1}</text>
                <text class="ts" x={rotX(i) + 60} y={row.y + 54} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="font-size:10.5px">{l2}</text>
              </g>
            ))}
            {row.boxes.slice(1).map((_, i) => (
              <line x1={rotX(i) + 121} y1={row.y + 35} x2={rotX(i + 1) - 2} y2={row.y + 35} stroke="#a32d2d" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#abig4)"/>
            ))}
            {Object.entries(row.anchors).map(([k, [title, line]]) => {
              const x = rotX(Number(k));
              return (
                <g>
                  <line x1={x + 60} y1={row.y + 118} x2={x + 60} y2={row.y + 73} stroke="#993556" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#abig4)"/>
                  <g class="c-pink">
                    <rect x={x} y={row.y + 118} width="120" height="44" rx="9" stroke-width="1"/>
                    <text class="th" x={x + 60} y={row.y + 133} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="font-size:12px">{title}</text>
                    <text class="ts" x={x + 60} y={row.y + 150} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="font-size:10.5px">{line}</text>
                  </g>
                </g>
              );
            })}
          </g>
        ))}

        <line x1="120" y1="602" x2="154" y2="602" stroke="#a32d2d" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#abig4)"/>
        <text class="ts" x="164" y="602" dominant-baseline="central" fill="#475569" style="font-size:11px">checks the next stage before it runs</text>
        <line x1="420" y1="602" x2="454" y2="602" stroke="#993556" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#abig4)"/>
        <text class="ts" x="464" y="602" dominant-baseline="central" fill="#475569" style="font-size:11px">checked against a key the OEM cannot change later</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-brand-muted">
        On Intel, the ACM does every check up to the IBB, then the IBB runs and checks the rest. On AMD, once Platform
        Secure Boot is enabled, fuses bind the processor to the OEM's signing key for good. Sources:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/software-security-guidance/resources/key-usage-in-integrated-firmware-images.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Intel key usage</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://tianocore-docs.github.io/Understanding_UEFI_Secure_Boot_Chain/draft/secure_boot_chain_in_uefi/intel_boot_guard.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>Boot Guard chain</a>,{" "}
        <a href="https://www.amd.com/content/dam/amd/en/documents/products/processors/technologies/amd-pro-technologies-security-white-paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>AMD PRO security</a>.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-semibold text-brand-text">UEFI in six phases</h3>
    <ol class="mb-4 space-y-2">
      {uefiPhases.map(([abbr, name, body]) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-0.5 inline-flex h-6 min-w-10 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full px-1.5 font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-white" style="background:#0f6e56">{abbr}</span>
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-brand-body"><strong class="text-brand-text">{name}.</strong> {body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <p class="mb-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-brand-muted">
      On an Arm board, TF-A has already done the reset and DRAM work, and EDK2 starts as BL33. Embedded boards often run
      U-Boot instead, which implements the subset of UEFI that Arm's{" "}
      <a href="https://arm-software.github.io/ebbr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>EBBR</a> requires. Secure Boot is optional there. Source:{" "}
      <a href="https://www.tianocore.org/tianocore-wiki.github.io/development/design-architecture/pi_boot_flow.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>TianoCore PI boot flow</a>,{" "}
      <a href="https://docs.u-boot.org/en/latest/develop/uefi/uefi.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={link}>UEFI on U-Boot</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-semibold text-brand-text">Legacy BIOS vs UEFI</h3>
    <div class="mb-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[640px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-brand-border text-left text-brand-text">
