    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ====== FIRMWARE TO KERNEL ====== -->
  <section class="mb-14">
    <p class="mono-eyebrow mb-2">// firmware --&gt; kernel --&gt; init</p>
    <h2 class="glitch mb-4 text-xl font-semibold text-brand-text" data-text="From the loader to the kernel">From the loader to the kernel</h2>
    <p class="mb-6 text-brand-body">
      Arm and PC boots meet here: BL33 or the UEFI firmware hands over to an OS loader, which starts the kernel.
    </p>

    <figure class="mb-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-brand-border bg-white p-4 sm:p-6">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 440" class="soc-svg mx-auto block h-auto w-full max-w-[980px] min-w-[860px]" role="img" aria-labelledby="kb-title kb-desc">
        <title id="kb-title">From the firmware to the real root filesystem, with the Secure Boot checks</title>
        <desc id="kb-desc">A row of six stages: the firmware (BL33 on Arm, UEFI on a PC), shim, GRUB or systemd-boot, the kernel, the initramfs, and the real root filesystem running systemd. Shim and GRUB form step 7, the OS loader; the kernel is step 8; the initramfs and the real root are step 9. Red arrows show the checks: the firmware checks shim against db and dbx, shim checks GRUB against the vendor certificate or a MOK, shim checks the kernel when GRUB asks it to, and dm-verity can check the real root. A dashed path shows the firmware starting the kernel directly, through the EFI stub or U-Boot with a signed FIT image. Below, the initramfs, command line and devicetree or ACPI tables are handed to the kernel, and the kernel checks the signature of every module it loads.</desc>
